and it said on screen, explicitly, used&nbsp;
with permission such-and-such films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.120
And I thought, you know, that's odd.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:59.280
The greatest astronomer of the time, Tico&nbsp;
Brahe, developed a new geocentric model:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.800
the earth occupies the center, the planets&nbsp;
orbit the sun, and the sun orbits the earth.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.880
You know, YouTube culture being what it is,&nbsp;
it's already unusual for anyone to actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:20.560
ask permission, let alone on flat earth YouTube.&nbsp;
So right away that told me that something was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:28.080
up. Clearly the people who made this first&nbsp;
video, which was just an anti-science screed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:36.320
on a channel full of flat earth, anti-vax, ancient&nbsp;
alien stuff, clearly all of these people were on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:43.360
the same wavelength. And that's weird. There's not&nbsp;
usually a lot of crossover between random YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:50.160
cranks and the kind of people who are able to get&nbsp;
in touch with Kate Mulgrew's agent. So I prod a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:56.640
bit and it turns out there's a whole buck wild&nbsp;
story behind that. It had actually made a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:02.960
splash when the film first came out in, I think&nbsp;
