It’s not wrong. You haven’t lied. You’ve just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:25.920 --> 00:30:30.160
omitted a conclusion that you know&nbsp;
participants will disagree with.

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:34.960
You don’t do a total hack job where you&nbsp;
cut up what people are saying to make them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:34.960 --> 00:30:37.040
say the opposite of what they meant.

00:30:37.040 --> 00:30:42.080
I noticed she was sitting on her&nbsp;
sweet can I grab her sweet can oh

00:30:42.080 --> 00:30:48.080
You use framing and juxtaposition, the&nbsp;
Kuleshov effect, to manufacture consensus.

00:30:48.080 --> 00:30:53.120
You take two scientists, and in between them&nbsp;
you sandwich a guy with a fake doctorate who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:53.120 --> 00:30:56.640
wrote a book called “Galileo Was&nbsp;
Wrong”. Technically speaking you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:56.640 --> 00:31:02.880
don’t take any of the three out of context,&nbsp;
you aren’t lifting razor thin sound bites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:02.880 --> 00:31:08.880
but all three are energized, excited, and,&nbsp;
seemingly, in agreement with each other.

00:31:08.880 --> 00:31:12.880
This is a fine line, you haven’t made&nbsp;
anyone say something they didn’t,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:12.880 --> 00:31:19.760
you’ve just created the appearance that they&nbsp;
support ideas that they don’t. It’s a form of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:19.760 --> 00:31:26.080
misrepresentation that’s exhausting to litigate,&nbsp;
and honestly isn’t worth it beyond, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:26.800 --> 00:31:30.160
